+def _usable_vector(v, dim: int) -> bool:
+    """Is this embedding safe to store or query with?
+
+    struct.pack takes NaN and infinity without complaint, so a broken embedder
+    writes rows that look fine and then behave strangely: with a NaN in the
+    table every distance comparison against it is false, so the row is not
+    "ranked badly", it is unreachable - and nothing anywhere says so. An
+    all-zero vector is the other shape of the same problem, and it is the one
+    Jan documents: cosine divides by the norm, so a zero vector makes every
+    score NaN rather than merely inaccurate.
+
+    The width check matters for a different reason. facts_vec is declared
+    float[dim] at creation and sqlite-vec raises on a mismatch; the store
+    already handles the model CHANGING width by dropping the table, but a
+    single short row from a partial read would raise per-insert forever.
+
+    Cheap: one pass over 384 floats, on a path that has just run a transformer.
+    """
+    if not isinstance(v, (list, tuple)) or len(v) != dim:
+        return False
+    total = 0.0
+    for x in v:
+        if not isinstance(x, (int, float)) or isinstance(x, bool):
+            return False
+        f = float(x)
+        # NaN is the only value not equal to itself; the infinities are caught
+        # by the range test. math.isfinite covers both and is clearer.
+        if not math.isfinite(f):
+            return False
+        total += f * f
+    # Not `total == 0`: an all-but-zero vector normalises to garbage just as
+    # badly, and float error means an exact zero is not the only way to get
+    # there. This threshold is far below any real unit vector, whose norm is 1.
+    return total > 1e-12
+
